\textbf{The three-arm grid on one corpus.}
Three arms answered ten gold questions over the five academic libraries of
the evaluation corpus (79 retrievable papers) through the public chat API,
on one model, with one prompt template, one 12-turn budget and one output
contract
(\path{benchmark-suite/results/runs/}
\path{run-20261013T094350Z-5f9faac}, graded by
\path{benchmark-suite/scripts/107_exp_grade.py};
Table~\ref{tab:e5-arms}, Figure~\ref{fig:threearms}). Arm~P (platform)
carries the knowledge-base read tools and no file tools --- the
leakage-free platform arm. Arm~F (bare agent) carries plain file tools
over the corpus and no index. Arm~D (dense discovery) carries a single
vector operation over the mirror index. The prompt differs between arms in
nothing but the tool list it declares.

\textbf{All three arms ground; only one cites.}
Every one of the 30 cells answered and every one named the gold document:
the grid cannot separate the arms on grounding, and three thousand cells
per arm would be needed to try. The arms separate on the second and third
readings. Arm~P passes both gates on all ten questions (10/10 key facts,
9/10 citations). Arm~D grounds and covers key facts just as well but cites
in 2 of 10 cells; where it does name its evidence it names a chunk ---
chunk-index markers appear seven times --- and a chunk is not a document a
reader can follow. Arm~F lands between them (7/10). Two key-fact failures
are morphological: a question about dynamic environments is answered with
``dynamic'' inside a compound, and the word-boundary test wants it standing
alone (\texttt{BQ09}); one of the citation misses has the same shape.
\emph{A retrieval stack can be right about the evidence and wrong about how
the evidence is addressed}, and the addressing layer is what a reader can
audit.

\textbf{What the separation costs.}
Arm~P is slower and dearer: 52.6\,s and \$0.711 per question against
22.2\,s/\$0.487 (F) and 24.5\,s/\$0.527 (D), \$7.11 of the \$17.25 the
grid spent. The gaps are consistent rather than noisy: paired by question,
arm~P is slower on 10 of 10 and dearer on 9 of 10, by $+30.4$\,s
($t = 4.54$, paired) and $+\$0.224$ ($t = 4.84$). The extra is not the
model or the question --- all three arms buy the same model on the same
questions --- it is the turns the platform spends reading and verifying
what it retrieved before it answers. The cheaper arms save turns by
checking less, and the dense arm's 3.7 tool calls per question say where
the saving comes from.

\textbf{File tools are a retrieval strategy, and on a small corpus a viable
one.}
Arm~F reaches the same grounding with plain \texttt{Read}/\texttt{Grep}
over 79 papers, and on this corpus that is not an accident: a local disk
and a corpus that fits one directory make scanning a real alternative to
indexing. It does not carry to the deployment corpus, where the same
question family needs the retrieval lanes --- 322 stored documents across
five libraries, and shelves an agent cannot enumerate by hand. We report
the small-corpus result because it bounds the claim: the platform's
advantage in this grid is auditability and citation discipline, not recall.

\textbf{Abstention is a property of the reading-and-answering layer, not of
a threshold.}
Three out-of-corpus questions produced three explicit not-found reports
(47.9, 73.3 and 113.5\,s wall clock; turns not recorded for this probe
family; \path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp8b_absent_agent.json}), each
enumerating the scope it searched (five libraries, 42 documents). The
retrieval tools themselves never abstain: six graded probes
(\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp8_graded_probes.json};
$n = 6$; three partially covered, three absent) retained 10 to 12 of their
12 candidates at every tier, with top scores up to 0.983 on absent probes
and 0.965 on partial ones; the 0.5 threshold never fired as a refusal
(Figure~\ref{fig:ood}).
Honesty is enforced where the answer is written, in the lane's
read-then-answer contract \cite{selfrag,feng-abstain,yin-knowwhat}, not by
a similarity cut-off.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig7_ood}
\caption{Out-of-corpus probes at the tool layer
(\protect\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp3_abstention.json}): for every probe
the judged hybrid keeps 11, 11, 9 and 11 of the 42--43 candidates its lanes
merged and dense
retrieval passes chunks above the 0.35 threshold (top vector scores
0.397--0.596, above it for 4, 8, 10 and 7 of the graded pool), so the
threshold alone never abstains and the 0.5 refusal threshold never fires. At
the agent layer, three absent-tier probes --- a separate family
(\protect\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp8b_absent_agent.json}) --- each
returned an explicit not-found report with zero fabricated citations.}
\label{fig:ood}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{Where the time goes.}
In the twelve grounded cells, machine-side retrieval and verification
stayed sub-second (Table~\ref{tab:retrieval}) against an end-to-end wall
clock of 36--91\,s. The gap is model turns: 11.7, 12.8 and 15.3\,s per
turn for the Search, Librarian and Hybrid arms --- the turn-dominated
profile reported for agentic retrieval \cite{singh-agentic-rag}, and the
reason orchestration runs server-side with a turn cap. Results repeat;
wall clock does not: Search-Q2 took 70\,s over 6 turns in round 1 and
165\,s over 11 turns in round 2, Librarian-Q4 84\,s over 6 turns and 92\,s
over 11.

