\textbf{Engines and delivery channels.}
The same task set on three engines separates by one to two orders of
magnitude in cost at one quality tier (Table~\ref{tab:e5-cost}): all 18
runs gold-hit with no fabricated citation, from \$0.03--0.07 per run on the
cheapest harness to \$0.9--1.8 on the agent surface. The cheapest engine
pays a 50--90\,s harness cold start per call, so its wall clock is about
twice its engine time. A timestamped replay then sent the same three modes
through two delivery channels: each passes 8 of 9 gates, and both fail the
same cell --- a false refusal reproduced in both, in the streaming channel
after 300.0\,s inside a single \texttt{kb\_search\_vector} call
(353.5\,s wall), so a stall was reported as absence rather than retrieved
around. Channel~3 removes the model layer and returns the gold in all 12
method--question cells in 10.2--37.3\,s. We report this as a dated
observation on this corpus.

\subsection{E5b: Deployment-layer system arms through the public API (2026-10-12 campaign)}
\label{sec:sysarms}
E1--E5 exercise the platform from the operator side. This campaign asks the
complementary deployment question: invoked exactly as an external caller would
invoke it, does the system still deliver the same contracts? Three questions
(two in-corpus: an English methodology question on the computer-science shelf
and a Chinese cross-lingual clinical question on the life-sciences shelf; one
out-of-corpus probe) crossed three system arms through the official external
chat API: arm~A pins the target shelf (vector fast lane), arm~B leaves the
library unpinned (librarian spine), arm~C pins the shelf and appends a
hybrid-retrieval directive. Every arm is gated mechanically; the gate is
refusal-first (an in-corpus arm fails if the answer constitutes a refusal),
because an earlier marker-only gate was fooled by refusal text that restates
the question keyword---the correction is part of the campaign record.

Table~\ref{tab:sysarms} reports the streaming channel; an independent
non-streaming replication returns the same 8/9 verdict. Arms A and C pass all
in-corpus questions with gold-cited answers (the InstructDS three-step
synthesis pipeline quoted verbatim from the gold document; the stroke risk
factors with cohort size 29{,}072) and all three arms abstain honestly on the
out-of-corpus probe. Mean end-to-end cost per question is \$0.182 (A),
\$0.300 (B) and \$0.219 (C). A same-questions pure-retrieval matrix without
an answering LLM hits gold in 6/6 mode-arms and 6/6 baseline arms (BM25,
dense and RRF all rank gold first); relative to the 2026-09-26 measurement,
the librarian lane improved from 342/234\,s to 25.0/10.2\,s
(13.7--22.9$\times$) and the hybrid lane from 1152/1818\,s to 37.3/33.7\,s
(30.9--54.0$\times$) after the mechanized-scan and GPU-judging rework.

The campaign also surfaced a new failure mode. Arm~B falsely refused the
computer-science question in three independent trials (two false refusals of
353.5\,s and 41.4\,s, one 600\,s hang). Session-transcript forensics locate
the mechanism: the 131-document shelf catalog returned by the librarian's
listing tool measures 74.9\,KB, which exceeds the agent engine's tool-output
budget; the engine persists the full payload and passes only a 2\,KB preview
into context, so the model never saw the gold entry. Shelves of 53 and 75
documents (33.1\,KB and 46.7\,KB) stay under the budget and succeed, and a
scripted librarian---a lexical scan over the same catalog outside the
engine's budget---answers the same question in 25.0\,s/10.2\,s with both gold
hits. The failure is a transport limit, not a ranking error, and it
generalizes this paper's thesis by one layer: the enforcement layer that
honesty requires has transport constraints of its own
(Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).

\textbf{The repair, and re-verification.} A 2026-10-13 campaign fixed the
transport in the tool contract and re-verified. Every catalog response is now
budget-guarded and paginated server-side (about 28\,K characters per page
with an explicit truncation marker and page count), a grouping view returns
the 131-document shelf as a 571-character map, and the previously failing
cell, re-run through the same public API, passes its gate (53.5\,s, nine
turns). Two further questions on the production library then exercised the
repaired lane on live content through the same public API: a transformer
question answered with a verbatim gold citation (98.5\,s, nine turns) during
which the agent requested a 100-document page, the guard clamped the page to
the budget in flight, and the run continued through the pagination contract;
and a cooking question the production library cannot support was refused
after a catalog-level scan (39.9\,s, five turns). No tool return in either
session was truncated to a preview. The incident closes the
loop this paper argues for: the enforcement layer that honesty requires was
undersized at its own transport; the failure was reproduced, root-caused in
the artifacts, repaired where every caller inherits the fix, and re-gated.

\textbf{An adversarial honesty battery (2026-10-13).} The abstention claim
was then stressed on purpose: eighteen arms --- three lanes $\times$ six
questions through the public API --- against ground truth machine-verified
\emph{before} the run. One question has its gold in the library; the five
verified-absent questions comprise two cooking questions, a same-word
different-sense trap (power-transformer selection against a shelf whose
\emph{transformer} documents are all the neural architecture), a fine-tuning
topic whose only library neighbours are near-miss documents, and a fabricated
protocol name. Outcomes: the in-corpus question is answered with verbatim
citations by all three lanes; all five verified-absent questions return
explicit not-found reports listing the scanned scope and their nearest
near-miss documents; and zero of the eighteen answers fabricate a citation.
Refusal latency scales with question ambiguity (35.8\,s for a clean
out-of-domain question against 217.4\,s for the in-domain-absent one). The
battery also separates lanes where more than one honest behaviour exists: on
the fine-tuning question the librarian lane refuses after judging six
near-miss documents (61 judged segments, none on-topic), while the hybrid
lane's vector arm recalls a body-level applied-usage document --- the
configured use of the technique literally appears in a
speech-representation paper --- and answers with the coverage boundary
stated inside the answer itself (\emph{no tutorial or primary paper exists
here; an applied configuration does}). Both behaviours are honest; a binary
found/refused gate cannot score the second, a grading limitation we report
rather than hide. Two arms that hit an intermittent tool-mounting race
reported ``tools unavailable, nothing fabricated'' and were re-run --- a
fail-closed behaviour counted as measurement-validity handling, not as a
retrieval outcome.

\textbf{A second-corpus replication of the honesty contracts.} The same
contracts then ran over the arXiv corpus on the ten-question gold set
(every question's gold verified present beforehand), in a
ten-question end-to-end pass whose every answer is retained
(the grid above, current) and, before it, in a smaller two-track
replication archived as a superseded campaign. In the current grid no
answerable question is falsely refused: all 30 cells answer, and every one
names its gold document. The contrast arm --- the same neutral prompt over
unverified three-chunk lists from BM25, dense and RRF, at gold recall 1.0
in the retrieval layer --- was measured in the archived replication: it
opened with an evidence-insufficiency
qualifier on six, six and four of the eight answerable questions
respectively even where its own list contained the gold --- the baseline
arm differs from the platform arm in prompt and evidence granularity as
well as in verification, so the contrast is an observation across delivery
configurations, not an attribution to the gate alone. Cost separated as
elsewhere in this section: \$0.79 per platform question in that
replication against \$0.05--0.08 per baseline generation.





