\subsection{E6: When the verifier fails}
\label{sec:e6}
\label{sec:inflation}\label{sec:eval-verify}   %% compatibility aliases (n2_related.tex:110)

\textbf{Same-domain score inflation.}
Across the sixteen hybrid tool runs (twelve candidates per run, eleven for
Q14's merge), the 191 judged candidates show no usable gold/non-gold
separation (Figure~\ref{fig:inflation}): every candidate scored at least
0.589, above the 0.5 acceptance
threshold, which rejects nothing here. The 17 in-pool golds (Q16's never
entered its pool) have median 0.88 against 0.86 for the 174 non-golds, a
difference a two-sided Mann--Whitney test cannot distinguish from chance
($p = 0.26$); only five of the 17 rank in the judged top three, nine eighth
or lower. The verdict as re-ranker therefore loses gold --- the judged
ordering reaches Hit@5 0.4375 (7/16; 0.44 in Table~\ref{tab:retrieval}) ---
while the verdict as a filter, or within rank fusion, holds 0.94
(Section~\ref{sec:e4}).

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_inflation}
\caption{Laya evidence scores for the 191 judged candidates of the sixteen
hybrid runs, split into the 17 in-pool gold documents and the 174
non-golds. Every candidate clears the dashed 0.5 acceptance line, so the
threshold rejects nothing on this corpus; the annotated group medians
(0.88 gold, 0.86 non-gold) sit within the spread of the two clouds, which
is why the two-sided Mann--Whitney test returns $p = 0.26$ rather than a
separation the gate could act on. Points are jittered horizontally for
legibility only.}
\label{fig:inflation}
\end{figure}

\textbf{The verdict is a model \cite{laya}, and a second model fails the
same way.}
The same 191 logged (query, candidate) pairs --- a replay, not a
re-retrieval --- went through a generic natural-language-inference
checkpoint: 330M parameters, one question-as-context template, premises
truncated to roughly the first 500 characters, 59 of the 191 pairs hitting
the cap (\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp7_second_signal.json}). It
scores below the verdict (Hit@5 0.3125, MRR 0.1375) with inverted
separation, gold median 0.0113 against non-gold 0.05285 ($p = 0.120$).
Truncation is not the cause --- six of the ten golds that fit the context
window whole still score below 0.01 --- nor domain: unrelated topics still
score corpus documents up to 0.9973, so an NLI threshold would abstain on
nothing either. A span-level replay inverts harder: over the 191 pairs and
3{,}489 span inferences at roughly 320 characters per span
(\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp9_span_nli.json}), max-pooling
gives 0.4728 for gold against 0.91855 for non-gold ($p = 0.0074$), Hit@5
0.1875; rank fusion repairs neither signal (Hit@5 0.3125, MRR 0.111458).
Replacing the judge checkpoint is not the repair.

\textbf{What the failure tracks.}
None of these alternatives reaches the verdict's quality, ruling out the
checkpoint as sole cause; and the second corpus shows the same gate losing
nothing there (Section~\ref{sec:e4}), so the collapse tracks confuser
density within one domain --- clusters of near-duplicates competing for the
same question. That probe moved domain, language and corpus size together
with the confuser clusters --- an association confounded by design, not an
elimination
\cite{zheng-llmjudge,panickssery-selfpref,lightman-verify,yin-knowwhat}.
The verdict earns its place as a filter and loses it as a ranker.



