\subsection{E7: Non-functional behaviour}
\label{sec:e7}
Stability, robustness and load behave within their contracts. A 27-cell
stability campaign (three tasks $\times$ three retrieval modes $\times$ three
rounds) completed with all 27 cells answered and none hallucinated
(\path{review/stability-multiround-20261013/results}); run-to-run spread is
wall clock, not content --- 14.9--182.0\,s across the grid, driven by turn
count, while the answers stay on the same evidence. A separate concurrency
sweep on the same instance (90/180/270 requests at 5/15/30 clients, zero
errors; supplementary material) reports \emph{p50} rising from 5.2 to
1577\,ms, and a vector-only run at 30 clients completed 159 of 270 requests
in 30\,s --- about 5.3 requests per second, a single-instance capacity
observation rather than a validated throughput figure. Concurrent write
phases in the same sweep reported no lost updates, but we have no raw counts
for them, so we do not claim durability under interruption. The retrieval
layer itself costs what Table~\ref{tab:retrieval} reports (two-stage 77\,ms
mean, 1.00 Hit@5 on this corpus), and load behaviour saturates gracefully
rather than failing: the librarian lane's CPU-bound
judging stages remain the latency floor, which the E5b mechanized-scan rework
addresses. Delivery surface moves per-question cost by more than an order of
magnitude --- $\$0.03$--$0.07$ per run on the cheapest harness against
$\$0.9$--$1.8$ on the agent surface (Table~\ref{tab:e5-cost}), and
$\$0.18$--$0.30$ on the industrial arms of E5b --- so the retrieval contract
is cheap to keep and expensive to fake.

\subsection{E8: Ablations of the verification gate and the search scope}
\label{sec:e8}

Two matched ablations disable, in a control arm, the two mechanisms the
design chapters argue for --- the content gate of Section~\ref{sec:gate}
and the content-routed partition of Section~\ref{sec:org-placement} --- on
the second corpus (ten questions over the five content-routed libraries,
322 stored parts), with nothing else changed, and both return a largely
null result, which is what makes them informative about which layer
actually binds. Both are ten-question grids of which eight have
a gold-matchable outcome. In the gate ablation three
arms shared one candidate pool (cap 12), one 4{,}000-character evidence
pack and one grader
(\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp11_gate_ablation.json}): no gate,
the gate as a filter, and a diagnostic arm receiving the judge's own
segment-level evidence pack. Gold was packed at rank 1 in all eight
gold-answerable questions in all three arms, and on nine of ten questions
the gated arms received exactly the documents the ungated arm received ---
so verification's measured value on this corpus is fail-closed provenance
(every returned document is judged), not ranking, and the residual loss
sits in the reading layer: the only cell the gate shortened was QK09 (12
to 4 documents, no grade change), the gated arms differed from the control
only where the answering model's own output varied (QK01 $0\to5$, QK03
$3\to0$; sign test $p = 1.0$, $n = 10$), and
QK01 is the clearest variance evidence --- both of its arms received the
same twelve documents and the same 4{,}000-character budget and graded 0
and 5. The segment-granularity arm scored 0.375 on the same subset, below
the control, losing three answers the document-level gate kept and gaining
one. Four of the eight gold-answerable questions (QK02, QK04, QK05, QK08)
scored zero in every arm and a fifth scored partial credit in one arm,
even though the gold document was packed at rank 1 in all of them; both
non-gold questions --- an unanswerable and an out-of-corpus probe --- were
answered correctly as abstentions in every arm, the same layer at which
the industrial corpus locates honest abstention (Section~\ref{sec:e5}).
The search-scope ablation
(\path{paper-kbs/experiments/results/exp12_partition_ablation.json}) varies
what the retriever may see rather than what survives it: pinned to the
gold library, Hit@5 is 1.0 with the gold at rank 1; pinned to a different
library, Hit@5 is 0.0 --- and the wrong library still yields surviving
candidates, so the failure is confident and
silent rather than an empty result; searching all five libraries at once
reproduces the gold-pinned result exactly, so partitioning costs no recall
on this corpus; the catalog path also reaches 1.0 but at a mean gold rank
of 1.75 and only after the gate has reduced 236 candidates to 11.4, with
catalog routing selecting the correct library first for 6 of the 8
questions. A misplacement is therefore not a ranking penalty but a
potential total recall loss --- and because every stored filename carries
the acquisition prefix
(\texttt{\{domain\}\_\_\{arxiv\_id\}\_\_\{slug\}}, present on 322/322
parts), name-based routing is not an independent measurement: what the
platform demonstrates is routing coverage, not routing accuracy. Both
ablations are single-corpus, single-engine, ten-question measurements,
reported as bounds rather than effect sizes. One protocol deviation is
material: the gate ablation was rebuilt around the judging tool applied to
a frozen shared candidate set, because the configuration switch named in
earlier platform documentation does not exist in the shipped tool layer;
the ungated arm is therefore a fusion-ordered control, only the within-run
contrast is comparable, and two of the thirty gate cells were re-run after
an authentication failure terminated them mid-run, the replacements
recorded alongside the originals.
